{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And he lodged there.</b> Against his will, for <b>the sun had set</b>, and as Rav Yehuda said: a person should always enter [a place] while it is yet good [daylight] and depart while it is yet good. But had the sun not set, he would have kept going as far as he was able, in order to get far from Esav." "<b>And he took from the stones of the place.</b> From the stones of the altar on which Yitzhak his father was bound, and Rashi explained that they all became one, and a hint is found in the Aggadah through them that his bed would be whole (i.e., he would marry all the tribes through one wife). And one may say: <b>and he took</b> one stone from among the stones of the place — and that is why it is written immediately, 'and he took the stone that he placed at his head.'" "<b>And he lay down in that place.</b> And not on cushions and bedding, even though he was near the city, as it is written, 'but Luz was the name of the city originally.' Rather, because he wanted to rise early in the morning, as it is written, 'And Avraham rose early in the morning.' And had he lodged inside the city, he would not have been able to depart at first light, for the gates are closed. Another interpretation: therefore he lay down outside the city, because the entrance to the city was hidden, as it is written in Judges regarding Luz: 'Show us, please, the entrance to the city' — and no person could stand at its gate."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {2 "The phrase נמצא באגדה רמז לו בהן שתהא מטתו שלמה may be slightly defective; 'a hint is found in the Aggadah' could also be read as 'the Aggadah found in them a hint.' The parenthetical gloss summarizes Rashi's explanation (ad loc.) that 'his bed would be whole' means Jacob would not marry any additional wives beyond Rachel."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:06:30.807361163Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}